# Coding Standards

Supports `.cursor/rules/.project-overview.mdc`. Follow `.cursor/rules/architecture.mdc` for layout and boundaries. These rules apply when writing or changing code.

## Naming

Use business-specific names. Suffix by role:

| Role | Pattern | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Use case | `<Verb><Noun>UseCase` | `CreateConversationUseCase` |
| Repository port | `<Noun>Repository` | `ConversationRepository` |
| Other port | `<Capability>Port` | `AIServicePort`, `LLMPort`, `EventBusPort` |
| Adapter | `<Tech><Noun>` | `PostgresConversationRepository`, `PythonAIServiceAdapter` |
| Event | `<Noun><PastTense>Event` | `ConversationEscalatedEvent` |
| Domain error | `<Noun><Problem>Error` | `InvalidConversationStateError` |
| Permission | `<resource>.<action>` | `conversation.escalate` |

Avoid `CommonService`, `HelperService`, `DataManager`, `GenericRepository`, and catch-all `utils` for business logic.

## Controllers and handlers

Inbound adapters stay thin: validate input → authenticate/authorize → call one use case → map the result.

Never put state transitions, SLA, assignment, escalation, RAG, prompt, or agent logic in controllers, WebSocket handlers, or webhook handlers.

## Validation

Validate every external boundary before domain/application logic runs:

- HTTP, WebSocket, webhooks
- Queue/event messages
- Uploaded files
- External API responses
- AI structured output

Never trust LLM output. Schema-validate it in Python (Pydantic) and again at the TypeScript boundary before a business use case consumes it. Reject or safely degrade invalid AI payloads; do not pass them through.

## Errors

Throw or return domain/application errors, not raw infrastructure exceptions:

- `ConversationNotFoundError`
- `UnauthorizedConversationAccessError`
- `InvalidConversationStateError`
- `TicketNotFoundError`
- `AIProviderError`
- `RateLimitExceededError`

Map these at the inbound adapter. Clients never receive stack traces, Prisma messages, provider payloads, or internal paths.

## Auth and tenancy

Authentication and authorization are separate. Check permissions server-side on every protected operation.

Enforce tenant context on every tenant-scoped read/write, including RAG retrieval. Propagate tenant context from TypeScript to Python on every AI/RAG call. Never rely on frontend filters. Never return or search across tenants.

## Security

- Secrets only via environment variables; document keys in `.env.example` with no real values
- Never hardcode API keys, JWT secrets, DB credentials, LLM keys, or webhook secrets
- Rate-limit sensitive APIs
- Validate webhooks
- Do not log secrets, tokens, raw prompts with PII, or unnecessary customer content
- Restrict AI tools to an explicit allowlist; authorize each tool call before executing a use case
- Guard against prompt injection; never let model output bypass validation or auth

## AI coding rules

- Implement AI in Python (`apps/ai`): orchestration, agents, RAG, embeddings, vector search, LLMs, prompts, tools, guardrails, evaluation
- TypeScript `modules/ai` is integration-only: `AIServicePort` + `PythonAIServiceAdapter` + contracts
- TypeScript must not import or use OpenAI SDK, Anthropic SDK, LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex, embedding SDKs, vector-DB SDKs for AI, prompt orchestration, or AI agents
- TypeScript does not import Python AI internals; communicate via HTTP, queues/events, and `packages/contracts`
- Python AI adapters do not mutate business state or call Prisma
- Tool flow: Python LLM request → TypeScript validates/authorizes → TypeScript use case → domain → return tool result to Python
- Keep prompts, tool schemas, and model IDs out of TypeScript domain entities; they live in Python
- Schema-validate AI output in Python (Pydantic) and again at the TypeScript boundary
- Do not install every AI provider up front; adapters are chosen later
- AI feature → Python; business feature → TypeScript. Do not invert that.

## Observability

Use structured logs with a correlation id. Include when known: `requestId`, `tenantId`, `conversationId`, `messageId`, `eventId`, `jobId`.

For AI calls also log model, token usage, latency, and cost — not full prompts or completions unless explicitly required and redacted.

## Tests

Colocate tests with the layer they protect:

- Unit: entities, value objects, domain services, use cases
- Integration: repositories, queues, outbound adapters
- E2E/API: auth, authorization, tenant isolation, contracts, core workflows
- TypeScript (Vitest): domain, application, API, repositories, integration, event processing; tool authorization and invalid-AI-payload rejection at `AIServicePort`
- Python (pytest): AI domain, orchestration, agents, RAG, retrieval, prompts, tool calling, guardrails, LLM adapters, AI evaluation; fake `LLMPort` / `EmbeddingPort` / `VectorSearchPort`

Do not test Prisma internals through domain tests. Do not hit real LLM providers in unit tests; fake ports. Do not put prompt, LangChain, LangGraph, or LlamaIndex tests in `apps/api`.

## Frontend

Organize by feature (`conversations`, `tickets`, `customers`, `knowledge`, `agents`, `analytics`). Call TypeScript backend APIs/WebSockets only. No direct database, Redis, Python AI, or private AI-provider access from the client. No AI logic in React.

## Shared code

Add to shared only if it is cross-cutting: `Result`, `DomainError`, `Logger`, `RequestContext`, `Pagination`, `EventBus` (`packages/shared`). Cross-runtime DTO / API / event contracts belong in `packages/contracts`. Module-specific helpers stay in that module.

## Config

Read runtime config from the environment. Fail fast on missing required secrets in production. Never commit `.env`.
